  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday) \nIssued at 1137 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough is progged to sweep across the area Monday \nnight into Tuesday, bringing increased potential for precipitation\nto West Central Texas. The focus of the long term forecast remains\non the potential for a possible wintery mix during the Monday \nnight into Tuesday time frame. Confidence still remains fairly \nlow given that the models continue to struggle with how cold \ntemperatures will get and the exact precipitation type that will \nfall. However, it is notable that over the past 24 hours, models \nare starting to slightly trend toward more coverage across the \narea with this wintery mix solution, so we will continue to \nmonitor this closely. It is also worth noting that the NBM has \nstarted showing signs of a wintery mix developing across the the \nwestern portions of the Big Country and northwestern Concho Valley\nTuesday morning. \n\nSurface high builds into the area for Wednesday and Thursday,\nsetting the stages for the first freeze of the season for many\nlocations. Current forecasts show lower to mid 30s across the \narea, and are likely not handling the radiational cooling set up \nthat is possible. Models continue to struggle with the track of \nthe next upper-level system for late week (Friday-Saturday), with\nthe GFS still showing closed system slowly tracking across the\narea late Friday morning and fairly slower than other models. \nWhereas the ECMWF is faster and weaker with the upper level \nsystem. Models are showing a gradual warming trend by the end of \nnext week and through the weekend, with high temperatures reaching\nback into the mid to upper 60s by Sunday.",
